<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margiana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margiana was a historical region centred on the oasis of the Murghab river in modern Turkmenistan, around the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>. Home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> sites such as Gonur Tepe, it later formed a province of the Achaemenid, Seleucid and Parthian empires and was a key stage on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margiana.jpg" alt="Margiana" width="250" height="115" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self, based on WP locator maps Category:Locator maps · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margiana-300BCE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Oxus civilisation is the common name for what archaeologists formally call the Bactria–<b>Margiana</b> Archaeological Complex (BMAC), a Middle Bronze Age civilisation of southern Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Their irrigation technology had first developed in the Bactria-<b>Margiana</b> Archaeological Complex west of the Pamirs, though how it reached the Tarim is unclear, and their staple crops, wheat and barley, also came from the west.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indo-Aryans are seen as a branch that diverged from other Indo-Iranian tribes at the Andronovo horizon, which arose from the Sintashta culture, and that moved through the Bactria–<b>Margiana</b> area of present-day northern Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory, later called Dahistan, bordered the farming lands of Hyrcania and <b>Margiana</b>, and they traded and raided along this edge.</blockquote><a href="/state/dahae">Dahae</a></li></ul></section>
